# Coverage scope for the 100% gate in .github/workflows/ci.yml.
#
# The gate is 100% on the code that SHIPS. That is a real standard and it stays at 100 --
# lowering the number would be the loosening this repository refuses everywhere else. What this
# file does instead is say precisely which code the number is about, and why anything outside it
# is outside.
#
# EXCLUDED, with reasons that are checkable rather than convenient:
#
#   tradecraft/embed_find.py
#     The embedding find stage. MEASURED AT CHANCE on 2026-08-27
#     (eval/RESULTS-2026-08-27-embedding-find-stage.md) and reported as a negative result: it
#     does not work, nothing in the shipping path calls it, and its dependencies (numpy,
#     sentence-transformers) are deliberately absent from requirements.txt, which promises the
#     core grader and its tests need only the stdlib. So it cannot even be imported in CI.
#     Testing a module that is measured not to work, in order to move a number, is the
#     definition of a green build bought rather than earned -- and a fake-model suite written to
#     do exactly that was deleted on 2026-09-03 rather than committed.
#     THE TRIGGER TO DELETE THIS EXCLUSION: the module is either fixed and put on the shipping
#     path, or removed. Neither has happened. Until one does, it is dead weight the gate should
#     not be pretending to measure.
#
# Everything else in the package is at 100%, including the paths reached only by adding tests
# for them on 2026-09-03: corpus enumeration's malformed-record and unreadable-file branches,
# the Gould-Fernandez role census, verify-mode validation, suffix-absorption limits, and typed
# graph-edge passthrough. Those were real branches with quiet failure modes, not filler.

[run]
source = tradecraft
omit =
    tradecraft/embed_find.py

[report]
fail_under = 100
show_missing = True
exclude_lines =
    # CLI entry points are exercised by running the tool, not by importing it. Excluding the
    # guard line itself, never the functions it calls.
    if __name__ == .__main__.:
    # Defensive branches that are unreachable through the public API. `spread()`'s exhaustion
    # break is the one case: it can only run when the requested limit exceeds the corpus, and
    # the early return at the top of the function already handles that. Kept in the source
    # because a future caller could reach it; not counted, because today nothing can.
    pragma: no cover
